{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1090557-council-of-traditions-and-technocracy-guides","id":1090557,"title":"Council of Traditions and Technocracy Guides?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","World of Darkness","Mage: The Ascension"],"posts":[{"date":"2017-05-09T19:28:27-07:00","id":1090557,"title":"Council of Traditions and Technocracy Guides?","content":"Hi, I was wondering, are there supplemental books which provide you with everything you need to know about the Council of Traditions and the Technocracy without actual reading the Core Rulebook? I ask because the M20 Edition of Mage the Ascension is very expensive, and not something I can get &quot;right now&quot; if I wanted to without badly denting my disposable income.<br /> <br /> What I mean is, books which provide the basic premise and themes of those factions, and particularly, books which feature all of them in one book.(a book with all the traditions, and a book with all the conventions) rather than a book for each Tradition/Convention.<br /> <br /> Can I assume that Guide to The Technocracy is one such book?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":9207,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9207-lord-proteus","user":"Lord Proteus","date":". 05-09-2017, 11:36 PM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9207-lord-proteus","name":"Lord Proteus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T19:50:12-07:00","id":1090565,"title":"","content":"Both the Guide to the traditions and players guide to the technocracy give a more nuanced look in how each organization operates with laws and leadership structures. <br /> <br /> In the guide to the traditions it has a section that explains what each tradition brings to the whole from the view point of said tradition which is nice.<br /> <br /> Now both of these are revised books so they won't have M20 mechanics in them","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/13009-burritomage","name":"BurritoMage","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar13009_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"585"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T20:41:48-07:00","id":1090578,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Lord Proteus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1090557-council-of-traditions-and-technocracy-guides#post1090557\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Hi, I was wondering, are there supplemental books which provide you with everything you need to know about the Council of Traditions and the Technocracy without actual reading the Core Rulebook? I ask because the V20 Edition of Mage the Ascension is very expensive, and not something I can get &quot;right now&quot; if I wanted to without badly denting my disposable income.<br /> <br /> What I mean is, books which provide the basic premise and themes of those factions, and particularly, books which feature all of them in one book.(a book with all the traditions, and a book with all the conventions) rather than a book for each Tradition/Convention.<br /> <br /> Can I assume that Guide to The Technocracy is one such book?</div> </div> </div> </div>The Core Rulebooks don't actually delve all that deeply into their group. They give you enough to understand them so you can play them but not a whole lot more than that. Though M20 does give more on the groups than previous rulebooks did.<br /> <br /> <i>Guide to the Traditions</i> and <i>Guide to the Technocracy</i> give the rundown of the two groups but they don't really go into the individual Traditions and Conventions and explore what makes them tick because that's what the Tradition/Convention Books cover.<br /> <br /> If you're familiar with VtM, think of it like their Guides to the Anarchs, Camarilla and Sabbat verses the Clan Books.<br /> <br /> So yeah, you're rather out of luck if you're looking for one Big Book on the Traditions/Conventions because while there is overlap between what the Guides cover and what the Trad/Con Books cover, there's a great deal that isn't covered.<br /> <br /> For example, <i>Guide to the Technocracy</i> gives a general history of the Technocracy and the Conventions from the Union's point of view while the individual CBs will go into the history of their respective Convention, talking about things that may only be mention in passing in the Guide or not at all, from the point of view of that Convention. Further the CB will talk about the organization of that Convention, it's major leaders, what it's going around the world, it's opinions on its fellow Conventions, the Traditions and other things, etc.<br /> <br /> Honestly, while the Guides are probably what you want, they aren't going to give you the full picture alone, all the nuance of the various Traditions and Conventions. For that you're ultimately going to need the Guides <i>and</i> the Trad/Con Books.<br /> <br /> Get the Guides first and go from there. Though if you're not adverse to PDFs and spending more right now to save money in the long term, DriveThruRPG does have a pair of bundles that might interest you. The <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/187968/Technocratic-Union-BUNDLE\" target=\"_blank\">Technocratic Union Bundle</a> has every Technocracy book currently out. Guide to the Technocracy, the Revised Edition Convention Books, the bundled First Edition Convention Books and the Technomancer's Toyboy which actually isn't solely Technocracy book as it provides tools and devices for any and all science-based Willworkers. Though the latter couple of books are pretty dated. You can still get things out of them but they're from a different age. <br /> <br /> And then there is the <a href=\"http://www.drivethrurpg.com/product/134717/Mage-Revised-Traditions-BUNDLE?src=also_purchased\" target=\"_blank\">Mage Revised Traditions Bundle</a> which is the ten Revised Edition Tradition Books, the Nine Traditions and the independent Hollow Ones.<br /> <br /> Like I said, you'll pay more for them right now with the bundles but it will actually be cheaper than buying them all individually over time. But it's up to you.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://youtu.be/_yBS2EZFduI?t=2m13s\" target=\"_blank\">Homo sapiens. What an inventive, invincible species. It's only a few million years since they crawled up out of the mud and learned to walk. Puny, defenceless bipeds. They've survived flood, famine and plague. They've survived cosmic wars and holocausts. And now, here they are, out among the stars, waiting to begin a new life. Ready to outsit eternity. They're indomitable. Indomitable.</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/420-akatsukileader13","name":"AkatsukiLeader13","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar420_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1637"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-09T20:45:47-07:00","id":1090580,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>BurritoMage</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-classic-world-of-darkness/mage-the-ascension/1090557-council-of-traditions-and-technocracy-guides?p=1090565#post1090565\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Both the Guide to the traditions and players guide to the technocracy give a more nuanced look in how each organization operates with laws and leadership structures.<br /> <br /> In the guide to the traditions it has a section that explains what each tradition brings to the whole from the view point of said tradition which is nice.<br /> <br /> Now both of these are revised books so they won't have M20 mechanics in them</div> </div> </div> </div>Thank you for the information, this will be helpful.<br /> <br /> I just want the background stuff, not the rules. I'm intending on integrating them into my merged Classic/Chronicles of Darkness setting, so of course I need to at least know a bit more about them than the surface details. I plan on getting the Translation Guide to go with them, and I do plan on getting the M20 Core Book(and the Technocracy Reloaded when it's released), but those are things I'll be getting much later.<br /> <br /> I plan on using the Technocratic Union as a rival faction, rather than full on villains/antagonists, who quarrel with the Pentacle/Traditions in the same way the Pentacle/Traditions sometimes contend with, bicker and challenge each other. That is, they are aware of each other, and have a mild amount of cooperation, and sometimes have issues with each others goals and way of doing things, but they are not actively malicious and hostile to each other.<br />","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/9207-lord-proteus","name":"Lord Proteus","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"25"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2017-05-10T02:30:49-07:00","id":1090649,"title":"","content":"Great advice above. If you were going to choose just one convention book for the Technocracy, I might choose the newest book for the NWO (dated 2012). If I am not mistaken, it's the first in the line of 'recent' Technocracy books, so it takes some time to give a picture of how things have changed from the Revised edition of the game (which launched in 2000 and closed out in 2004). That 8-12 years of technological development changes so much for the dynamics of the Technocracy and their rivals.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/12988-imperator","name":"Imperator","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar12988_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Feb 2017"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"41"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}